{"sequence": 5, "time_utc": "2026-11-10T06:17:33.701068+00:00", "kind": "model_call", "task": "half2_rewrite", "model": "gpt-5.4-mini-2026-03-17", "stage": "writer", "resolved_model": "gpt-5.4-mini-2026-03-17", "prompt_version": "stage-b-0.7-feedback-stability-v1-editorial-checks-v1", "prompt_hash": "4044bb7bfb9338eaafe08ba9ebd8f8a2aa32daf046dc413830a512cfa25f2fb0", "payload_hash": "83b55e1fbaebc3b89b0bea78fc76b320ed5b57f63969cb0fa5f6ec0d3b6ebf27", "schema_hash": "34ebde27d47aa44c7e7128cd27b29bb486b28f90959eac4209d20a86a4444e2e", "max_output_tokens": 4000, "reservation_tokens": 8207, "usage": {"input_tokens": 649, "output_tokens": 234}, "usage_basis": "reported", "charged_tokens": 24413, "elapsed_seconds": 1.8637864000629634, "response_id": "resp_00895c412fb2b70d016af2b6fc2bbc87d2b6d1908b99109871", "error_type": null, "response": {"paragraph": "Agreement with human judgments is one issue. Consistency across languages is another, and stability across repeated calls is a third. These aspects of an automated evaluator can come apart: a judge may produce stable decisions that are nevertheless incorrect, or agree with humans in one language but not on a meaning-matched version in another. We propose a controlled bilingual study that measures these properties separately in context-grounded short question answering. A feasibility pilot will contain 20 passage–question items, each with a correct and a deliberately incorrect answer in English and one additional language. Two fluent raters will independently assess each response before adjudication. One fixed language-model judge will evaluate the responses under English instructions, with an additional target-language instruction condition and three calls per condition. The analysis will separate human-reference agreement, cross-language decision changes, repeated-call stability, and non-decisions. The proposed contribution is a human-validated replication-and-extension protocol for a bounded QA setting, not a claim to introduce multilingual judging. The study is designed to distinguish incorrect judgments from unstable judgments and data-construction problems; no direction of effect is assumed."}}
